{"challenges":[{"attack":"Test whether the cardinality split misses an admissible subset: singleton {9} is absent, and any four distinct positive elements have sum at least 1+2+3+4=10; then test each remaining cardinality for omissions or duplicate listings.","claim_id":"count-seven","outcome":"survives","result":"Sizes 2 and 3 are the only possible sizes. The four listed pairs and three listed triples each sum to 9, are distinct, and the ordered-variable bounds exhaust both cases. Hence no subset is omitted or double-counted.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Test the displayed example directly for membership and sum.","claim_id":"example-subset","outcome":"survives","result":"Both 1 and 8 belong to the given set, and 1+8=9.","tool_ids":[]}],"checks":[{"basis_reasoning":"The claimed basis is derivation, and every supporting step can be verified directly. The size bounds exclude sizes 1 and at least 4; the pair and triple parameterizations exhaust the remaining sizes; and the two classes are disjoint by cardinality.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["size-bounds","pairs","triples","total-count"],"claim_id":"count-seven","reasoning":"The pair argument with a<b yields exactly a=1,2,3,4 and the four stated pairs. For triples a<b<c: a=1 permits b=2,3; a=2 permits b=3; and a>=3 gives sum at least 3+4+5=12. Thus there are exactly 4+3=7 subsets.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The claimed basis is derivation from the pair listing, and direct arithmetic verifies it.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["pairs"],"claim_id":"example-subset","reasoning":"{1,8} is listed among the exhaustive pairs and has sum 1+8=9.","verdict":"supported"}],"missing_evidence":[],"recommended_action":"finish","tool_requests":[]}
